¶
'''ague''' (ā′gū), ''n.'' [< ME. ''agu'', ''ague'', < OF. ''agu'', fem. ''ague'' (F. ''aigu'', fem. ''aigue''), = Pr. ''agut'', fem. ''aguda'', sharp, acute, < L. ''acutus'', fem. ''acuta'', acute, sharp, violent, severe; ''febris acuta'', a violent fever: see ''acute''.] '''1†.''' An acute or violent fever.
{{smaller block|And the burning ''ague'', that shall consume the eyes.
{{right|Lev. xxvi. 16.}}}}
'''2.''' Intermittent fever; a malarial fever characterized by regularly returning paroxysms, each in well-developed forms, consisting of three stages marked by successive fits, cold or shivering (the chill), hot or burning, and sweating; chills and fever.
{{smaller block|<poem>That ye schul have a fever terciane
Or an ''agu''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Nun’s Priest’s Tale, l. 140.}}}}
'''3.''' Chilliness; a chill not resulting from disease.—'''Dumb ague.''' {{smaller|See ''dumb''.}}

'''ague''' (ā′gū), ''v. t.'' [< ''ague'', ''n.''] To cause a shivering in; strike with a cold fit. ''Heywood''. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Faces pale
With flight and ''agued'' fear.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., i. 4.}}}}

